#CHELES Giorgio, 312CB

--History functionality--

In order to implement the undo/redo system, a stack made up of snapshots that
contain l-system, image and font is being used. Since we strive to keep memory
usage to a minimum the implementation uses a hybrid shallow copy/hard copy
approach. The element(s) that will be altered after calling an undoable command
are hard copied from the previous entry, then modified accordingly, the rest
are shallow copied, meaning only the variables are copied, pointers continue
to point to the same addresses as the previous entry.

UNDO - decrement the position in the stack unless its the first position.
REDO - increment the position in the stack unless its the last position and
       print confirmation message.

If user undoes action(s) and then enters an undoable command, the stack is
cleared after the current position, thus last position is set to the current
position.

Moreover, every snapshot has a code that indicates what action was last
performed, thus allowing to reprint confirmation messages when redoing, and
every element has a code that indicates if it empty, the original element,
or shallow copied over from the previous snapshot. Encoding like this enables
us to accordingly free memory during and at the end of the program.

--L-system implementation--

L-system are stored as a struct comprised of an axiom, an array of rules (a 
rule is a pair of a character and a character array), a number of rules, and
the name of the source file.

LSYSTEM <file> - Read L-system data from the specified file.

DERIVE <n> - Iterate through L-system axiom n times, each time replacing every
             character that can also be found in the rules array with its
             corresponding successor then print the result. The axiom is not
             modified after this action.

--Turtle graphics implementation--

An image is a struct that contains: width, height, pixel matrix, name of source
file.
A pixel contains three bytes that define R, G, B values.

LOAD <file> - Read image from the specified file. Since all input files are
              binary, a special approach is being used to reading the raster:
              the matrix of pixels is actually a width * height sized vector
              that has a pointers pointing to every <width> pixels. Using this
              we can read the image raster in one go, without iterating through
              height and width.
              Observation: Since the raster is one continous string of 0s and
              1s we must ensure that the pixel is packed, meaning it doesnt
              contain any padding junk. 

SAVE <file> - Save the image in the specified file using the PPM format, with
              a P6 header which means binary mode.

TURTLE <x> <y> <step> <orientation> <orientation_step> <n> <R> <G> <B> - The
    very first step is to get the nth derivation of the loaded L-system (If 
    L-system is not present then we do nothing). The so called "turtle" is set
    at (x, y) position, with its starting angle set to orientation. Next, a
    turtle stack is created that will contain a history of user saved positions
    and angles. Iterate through the derivation character by character and based
    on it we perform the following actions:

    F - We move the turtle forward by incrementing x and y using the following
        formulas: x += step * cos(orientation), y = step * sin(orientation).
        After the turtle is moved we draw a line between the turtle's previous
        position and its new position using a draw line function. The draw line
        function contains the draw pixel function that checks if the desired
        position to draw is out of bound or not. Using the RGB values, the
        pixel is set to the appropriate value.
    + - Increment the orientation with orientation step
    - - Decrement the orientation with orientation step
    [ - Add the corrent position and angle to the turtle stack
    ] - Get the last position and angle from the stack, load it into the turtle
        and pop that entry.
    other - Do nothing

At the very end, the turtle stack is cleared.

--Font implementation--

A font is a struct that contains: font name, name of source file, number of
glyphs and an array of glyphs.
A glyph is a struct that contains: glyph name, character code, width, height,
x bounding box offset, y bounding box offset, x cursor offset, y cursor offset,
glyph bitmap.

FONT <file> - Read the font from the specified file. The file format used is
              BDF, thus we read every file line, extract the first word, check
              if we need the attribute the word describes and store it. Since
              the file format allows user some flexibility, meaning the
              attributes can be written in any order, we cannot create a simple
              read function, instead we have to iterate through a registry of
              keywords of interest. The glyph is encoded in hexadecimals after
              the BITMAP keyword. We read <height> rows, two hexadecimals
              values at a time and place them in the bitmap matrix, whose width
              is rounded to the next multiple of 8 (to accomodate the 8 bits in
              two hexadecimal digits)

TYPE "<string>" <start_x> <start_y> <R> <G> <B> - Iterate through the <string>
    characters, search the character in the loaded font, and start drawing the
    bitmap on the loaded image from cursor_position + (x bounding box offset,
    y bounding box offset), which is the bottom left corner of the glyph, to
    the top left, iterating through every pixel on the bitmap. One a glyph is
    written, the cursor is incremented cursor_position += (x cursor offset, y
    cursor offset), thus allowing the next glyph to be written.

--Bitcheck implementation--

In order to identify 0010 and 1101 sequences in our series of 1s and 0s, 30
bits are analysed at a time. bits 0-2 are the last three bits of the previous
pixel, bits 3-26 are the current pixel's that is analysed whether it is at risk
of being misread or not, bits 27-29 are the first three bits of the next pixel.
We iterate through the pixels of the image, and at each step we extract the
binary data into our 30 elements long array of analyzed bits. Regarding the
edge cases where there is no last or next pixel, we simply ignore them when
analyzing. Taking 4 bits at a time allows us to check for the sought sequences,
and once we find one, we assess if changing 0010 to 0000 or 1101 to 1111 will
change our current pixel. If yes, we print the warning message. We then
scanning until we cant no more.


